# What did your opponent's last move do?
teaches: ["safety/threats","safety/three-questions"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "Why is reading the opponent's move the first habit of strong players? How do you spot a threat before it lands?"
next: {"title":"Does this exchange really win?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/03-does-this-exchange-really-win.cast"}

## Warm-up: loose pieces
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/3p4/n3p3/4P3/5N2/PPPBQPPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxa5]}
      - {fen: rnbq1rk1/pp2bppp/2p1p3/8/3NP3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qg4, Qxd4], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today's question: what did your opponent's last move do? First, a warm-up on loose pieces.
    draw position

B: Loose means nothing guards it. Fine, I'll find one.
    highlight piece_d2 piece_e2 piece_f3

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["Look at the edge of the board.","Your bishop on d2."] themes ["hanging piece"] voice a teaches ["safety/hanging-pieces"]
        right: Bishop takes a5: the knight on the rim had no guard.
        wrong: Find a black piece that nothing defends, then what reaches it.

B: The bishop, all the way from d2. I never look at the edge.
    point at.ref piece_a5 gesture tap

A: Now you're Black.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: White's queen has just jumped to g4.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.4

B: She's eyeing my king. I'd defend g7.
    point at.ref sq_g7 gesture circle

A: Your king already guards g7. Look at what she left behind.
    highlight piece_g8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["Where was the queen before?","Your queen on d8."] themes ["hanging piece"] voice a teaches ["safety/hanging-pieces"]
        right: Queen takes d4: the white queen used to guard that knight from d1.
        wrong: What did the white queen guard before she moved?

B: The knight on d4. The queen was its only guard.
    highlight piece_d4

A: One move ago that knight was safe. His last move made it loose.
    point at.ref sq_d1 gesture tap

A: That's today: how to read what his move changed, and how to see his threat before it lands.
    highlight piece_g4

## Three things a move changes
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1bqr1k1/p1p2ppp/8/1p2n3/8/5N2/PPP1QPPP/R1B2RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Nc4, b3, Rxe2], arrows: [{from: e8, to: e2}]}
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/pp2bppp/2p2n2/8/3P4/2NQ4/PPB2PPP/R4RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Nd5, Qxh7#], highlights: [h7]}
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/ppb2ppp/2p3n1/8/3PN3/1BP5/1P3PPP/R1Q2RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Qh4, Ng3], arrows: [{from: c7, to: g3}]}
      - {fen: r4rk1/ppb2ppp/2p3n1/8/3PN2q/1BP5/1P3PPP/R1Q2RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Qxh2#], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: nullmove
      title: Null Move Pruning
      authors: Chess Programming Wiki
      year: 2024
      finding: Engines try a 'null' (passing) move; the move that refutes the pass is called the threat move, and has been used to detect threats since the 1960s.
      url: https://www.chessprogramming.org/Null_Move_Pruning

    card title "Three things a move changes"

A: Now that loose pieces are warm, read Black's move.
    draw position

A: His knight jumps from e5 to c4.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.4

B: It's after my pawn on b2. I'll kick it: pawn to b3.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.4

A: And the rook takes your queen.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.4

B: Where did the rook come from?
    highlight piece_e2

A: From behind the knight. When the knight moved, it opened the e-file.
    draw arrow_0

A: Every move changes three things: what the piece attacks now,
    text t_attacks "attacks" x 900 y 460 font_size 44

A: what it stopped guarding,
    text t_leaves "leaves" x 900 y 390 font_size 44

A: and the line it opened.
    text t_opens "opens" x 900 y 320 font_size 44

    erase arrow_0

A: Here's another.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Black's knight leaves f6 for d5.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.4

B: It hits my knight on c3. I'll trade knights.
    point at.ref piece_c3 gesture circle

A: First ask what it stopped guarding.
    highlight t_leaves

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find the best move." widget chess line true hints ["What did the knight on f6 guard?","Your queen and bishop both aim at h7."] themes ["mating pattern"] voice a teaches ["tactics/mating-patterns"]
        right: Queen takes h7, mate: the bishop guards the queen, and the knight that held h7 has gone.
        wrong: Before you trade, look at the square the knight stopped guarding.

B: Mate. That knight was the only piece guarding h7 besides the king.
    draw hl_h7

    erase hl_h7

A: And one more question, the hardest: what does he want next?
    blocking false

    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    text t_next "next?" x 900 y 215 font_size 44

A: Black's queen comes to h4.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.4

B: It attacks my knight on e4. I'll move it.
    highlight piece_e4

A: Before you do, pretend he could move again. Swap chairs: you're Black.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Play his best move." widget chess line true hints ["His queen is not the only piece aiming at h2.","The bishop on c7 sees h2."] themes ["threat","mating pattern"] voice a teaches ["safety/threats","tactics/mating-patterns"] pool false
        right: Queen takes h2, mate: the bishop on c7 guards the queen, and White's own rook blocks f1.
        wrong: Taking the knight is good. Is there something even better?

B: Queen takes h2. That's mate.
    point at.ref piece_h2 gesture tap

A: Chess programs use the same trick, called a null move:
    text t_pass "pass" cites ["nullmove"] x 900 y 580 font_size 44

A: let one side pass, and see what the other threatens.
    highlight piece_h2

    erase t_pass

A: Back to your chair. Save the knight and stop the mate, with one move.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 1 duration 0.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Save the knight and stop the mate." widget chess line true hints ["One knight move does both jobs.","Block the bishop's line to h2."] themes ["defence"] voice a
        right: Knight to g3: it leaves the queen's reach and blocks the bishop's line, so h2 has one attacker and the king guards it.
        wrong: A check on f7 only loses the bishop. Moving the knight saves it, but only one square also stops the mate.

B: The knight blocks the bishop's line to h2.
    draw arrow_0

A: So after every move he makes: what does it attack, what did it leave, and what would he play if he moved again?
    highlight t_attacks t_leaves t_opens t_next

## Your turn: threat, then defence
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Five for five. Your opponents will hate playing you.
    good: Most of them. Swapping chairs gets quicker with practice.
    poor: Threats hide well at first. Swap chairs and look again.
    none: The puzzles will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r4rk1/pp3ppp/n1pb4/8/7q/2N5/PPP1QPPP/2BR1RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Qxh2#], flip: true}
      - {fen: r4rk1/pp3ppp/n1pb4/8/7q/2N5/PPP1QPPP/2BR1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxd6]}
      - {fen: r4rk1/ppp1qppp/8/2b5/3n4/1PN3P1/PBPQ1P1P/R4RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Nf3+, Kg2, Nxd2], flip: true}
      - {fen: r4rk1/pp1nbppp/1qp5/3p2B1/3P4/2N5/PPP2PPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxe7]}
      - {fen: r1bqr1k1/ppp2ppp/8/3p4/4n3/8/PPP1QPPP/R1B2R1K b - - 0 1, moves: [Ng3+, hxg3, Rxe2], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you've seen how a move changes the board: your turn. Find his threat first, then the defence.
    draw position

A: Black's queen has just come to h4. Swap chairs: you're Black, and it's your move again.
    highlight piece_h4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Play his threat." widget chess line true hints ["Two pieces aim at one square.","The queen and the bishop on d6."] themes ["threat","mating pattern"] voice a teaches ["safety/threats","tactics/mating-patterns"] pool false
        right: Queen takes h2, mate: the bishop on d6 guards her, and White's rook blocks f1.
        wrong: Look for a check that the king cannot escape.

B: Mate on h2 again. Queen and bishop.
    point at.ref piece_d6 gesture circle

A: Back in White's chair. Find the defence.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Stop the threat." widget chess line true hints ["What did his queen guard before she moved?","Your rook on d1."] themes ["defence","hanging piece"] voice a teaches ["safety/hanging-pieces"]
        right: Rook takes d6: the queen used to guard that bishop, and without it there's no mate on h2.
        wrong: Moving a pawn may stop the mate. Is there a move that stops it and wins something?

B: His queen left the bishop loose, so I take one of the attackers.
    highlight piece_d6

A: Next. Black's knight has just landed on d4. Swap chairs again.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Play his threat." widget chess line true hints ["A knight check.","Which square hits the king and the queen?"] themes ["threat","fork"] voice a teaches ["safety/threats","tactics/fork"] pool false
        right: Knight to f3, check: the king steps aside, and the knight takes the queen on d2.
        wrong: Find a knight check that also hits something big.

A: A fork with check. Seen in time, queen to d3 quietly stops it.
    highlight piece_d2

A: Back to White. Black's queen has gone to b6, after your pawns.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["What did the queen guard on d8?","Your bishop on g5."] themes ["hanging piece"] voice a teaches ["safety/hanging-pieces"]
        right: Bishop takes e7: the queen used to guard that bishop from d8.
        wrong: Before you save a pawn, ask what his queen stopped guarding.

B: His queen used to guard that bishop.
    point at.ref sq_d8 gesture tap

A: Last one. His rook stands behind his knight.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

A: Swap chairs one more time.
    highlight piece_e8 piece_e4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Play his threat." widget chess line true hints ["Move the knight with check.","Then the rook sees the queen."] themes ["threat","discovered attack"] voice a teaches ["safety/threats","tactics/discovered-attack"] pool false
        right: Knight to g3, check: White takes it, and the rook takes the queen on e2.
        wrong: Which knight move gives check and opens the e-file?

B: The knight checks, and the rook takes the queen.
    point at.ref piece_e2 gesture tap

A: So the danger is often not the piece that moved, but what it opened or left behind.
    fade position to 0.25

A: Ask what he'd play if he moved again.
    highlight piece_e8

    erase position

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower.
    text hw_game "one slow game: 15 + 10" x 500 y 430 font_size 56

A: Then find its first mistake.
    text hw_mistake "find mistake 1" x 500 y 340 font_size 56

## Where next
end_page: true

Next: Does this exchange really win?
    text end_prev_kicker "Previous" x 265 y 545 font_size 24 style.opacity 0.6
    link end_prev href lecture:1 title "Why have you stopped getting better?" size 320 x 265 y 395
    text end_next_kicker "Next" x 735 y 545 font_size 24 style.opacity 0.6
    link end_next href lecture:3 title "Does this exchange really win?" size 320 x 735 y 395

    link end_again href lecture:2 title "Watch again" form text open here x 500 y 160
    text end_count "2 of 30" x 500 y 95 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
